      "s": "Bloomberg Tech",
      "t": "Elon Musk’s SpaceX Starship: Why the Rocket Is Critical to Its Future",
      "d": "For the full experience visit: SpaceX’s Future Hinges on Starship. Can It Deliver?",
      "u": "https://www.bloomberg.com/graphics/2026-spacex-starship-rocket-future/",
      "m": "11. 13. PM 01:30",
      "ts": 1794576623000,
      "fullText": "Workers perform a fit check as SpaceX’s Starship is stacked onto the Super Heavy booster at Starbase in August 2021. Source: SpaceX SpaceX’s Future Hinges on Starship. Can It Deliver? By Loren Grush November 13, 2026 When Elon Musk first announced plans for a super-heavy, reusable rocket in 2012, his vision was to transport people to Mars . Today, he sees Starship as the backbone of his entire space business, and its $2 trillion market valuation hinges on the launcher’s technological and commercial success. The tallest and most powerful rocket ever built, Starship is meant to lift upgraded satellites for SpaceX’s booming Starlink internet-from-space venture and a fleet of up to a million spacecraft to serve as data centers for artificial intelligence, a project that underpinned the company’s blockbuster share sale this summer. On top of all of that, Starship is still tasked with realizing Musk’s lifelong dream of turning humanity into a “multiplanetary species.” It’s supposed to send people to the moon as soon as this decade, and then eventually to the red planet. Starship’s economic model relies on the rocket being able to land after a mission and quickly relaunch. Several technical hurdles remain before that can happen. The clock is ticking as the company is laying the groundwork to retire Falcon 9, the company’s workhorse rocket that transformed the economics of space. A Bigger Rocket Needs More Fuel SpaceX says Starship will be able to generate as much as 18 million pounds of thrust at liftoff — far more than the 7.5 million pounds generated by NASA’s Saturn V launcher that took people to the moon. While Saturn V lifted a bigger payload, Starship is heavier due to equipment needed to make it reusable. Each additional ton of payload inside the rocket requires several more tons of fuel to lift it, which adds more weight still and requires even more fuel. Longer Journeys Will Require Refueling in Orbit Reaching the moon or Mars will require refueling in space, some"
